/* CRT theme -- a green-phosphor monitor: near-black screen, one hue of green at
   several brightnesses, square corners everywhere, and a scanline texture over
   the chrome.

   Design rules this file follows, so later edits stay coherent:

   * One hue. Everything is the same green (h=140) at different value/chroma.
     #33ff88 is the lit phosphor, #1fa85c the half-lit, #12602f the unlit trace,
     #071108 the glass. Introducing a second hue breaks the illusion.
   * No rounded corners and no gradients -- a shadow mask draws squares. Every
     border-radius is 0 on purpose; do not "soften" them.
   * Text is never pure white. On a phosphor tube the brightest thing on screen
     is the phosphor itself, so the highlight colour IS the text colour and
     selection inverts to the dark glass instead.
   * Chrome and content surfaces use different scanline tiles. Each large
     surface owns one tile; child content stays transparent so adjacent rows and
     controls never restart the texture at mismatched phases.

   Status colours (connection failed / reconnecting) stay red/amber: those are
   painted from C++ and are semantic, not decorative. */

QWidget {
    background-color: #071108;
    color: #33ff88;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

/* The screen itself.

   About the scanline tiles (crt-scan-*.png): each is a 1x3 OPAQUE strip whose
   surface colour is baked in, and it is applied with the `background: url(...)
   repeat` shorthand and no colour of its own. That is not a stylistic choice --
   it is the only form Qt 5 renders correctly here, verified on screen:

     * background-color + background-image + background-repeat, written as three
       separate properties, applies the colour but paints the image exactly ONCE
       in the top-left corner. For a 1x3 tile that is invisible.
     * background: <colour> url(...) repeat tiles correctly but drops the
       colour, leaving the surface black.

   So the colour lives in the pixels instead. When changing a surface colour,
   regenerate its tile as well, or the two will disagree. */
QMainWindow, QDialog, QuickView#QuickViewSurface {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-screen.png) repeat;
}

QLabel[semanticState="error"] {
    color: #33ff88;
}

QLabel[semanticState="warning"] {
    color: #7ce8ac;
}

QLabel[semanticState="muted"] {
    color: #1fa85c;
}

/* The stack is only a page switcher. QuickView owns the stable tile origin and
   the default QLabel page is already transparent through the QLabel rule. */
QuickView#QuickViewSurface QStackedWidget#QuickViewStack {
    background: transparent;
}

/* Every other container that fills real estate. The QMainWindow rule alone is
   almost invisible in practice -- the two panels cover nearly all of it -- so
   the texture has to be named on the widgets that are actually on top, and Qt
   does not inherit a background-image the way it inherits a colour.

   These are class-name selectors, which Qt matches against
   QObject::metaObject()->className(); all of them declare Q_OBJECT.

   Text editors, trees and combo popups remain solid for dense text. File-list
   viewports and the command input are handled below through one stable owner
   per visible surface. */
FilePanel, TitleBar, FunctionKeyBar, CommandBar, BreadcrumbBar,
StatusBarWidget, FramelessDialog, QMessageBox, QTabBar, QToolBar,
QDialogButtonBox, QGroupBox {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
}

/* TitleBar and FramelessDialog paint their own shapes, so a `background:` rule
   cannot reach them -- they take the tile through qproperties instead. The
   dialog forwards the same tile to its DialogTitleBar. */
TitleBar, FramelessDialog {
    qproperty-backgroundTile: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png);
}

/* Bare QWidget containers that sit ON a textured parent. Qt paints the sheet's
   QWidget background onto these, which would cover the texture with a flat
   colour; transparent lets the parent's show through -- and keeps the scanlines
   in phase, which a second tiled copy would not (a background-image's origin is
   each widget's own top-left). */
QWidget#PanelAddressRow, QWidget#BreadcrumbSegments {
    background: transparent;
}

QMenuBar {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

QMenuBar::item {
    background: transparent;
    padding: 4px 10px;
}

/* Selection inverts, the way a block cursor does on a terminal. */
QMenuBar::item:selected {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}

QMenu {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

/* QWidgetAction rows sit above QMenu as real widgets. Keep their root surface
   transparent so the menu's scanline tile remains continuous underneath. */
QMenu QWidget[menuChromeSurface="true"] {
    background: transparent;
}

/* Colour spelled out rather than inherited, so a plain action can never fall
   back to the platform style's own menu-text colour. Note that Latin entries
   look dimmer than CJK ones at 1x -- thin strokes over a saturated green, not a
   colour difference: sampling "About this Program..." gives exactly #33ff88. */
QMenu::item {
    padding: 4px 24px;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QMenu::item:disabled {
    color: #12602f;
}

QMenu::item:selected {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}

QMenu::separator {
    height: 1px;
    background: #12602f;
    margin: 3px 0;
}

QToolBar {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    border: none;
    spacing: 2px;
}

QToolBar QToolButton {
    background: transparent;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    border-radius: 0;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QToolBar QToolButton:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
}

/* File views keep their palette colours for text and selection. Their actual
   viewport owns the sunken tile; ordinary detail rows and thumbnail cells are
   transparent, while selected/hover content is painted by each delegate. */
QTableView {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    alternate-background-color: #071a0d;
    gridline-color: #12602f;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    /* On the widget, not a ::item rule, so these fold into the palette for the
       delegate's fast path -- same reason as light.qss/dark.qss. */
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

/* QAbstractItemView delegates its styled background to the viewport during
   paint; styling the view class is therefore the reliable single owner. */
FileListView, IconFileView {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-sunken.png) repeat;
}

/* Inactive panel: the unlit trace, so the active panel's inverted row is
   unmistakably the one with focus. */
QTableView[panelActive="false"] {
    selection-background-color: #12602f;
    selection-color: #7ce8ac;
}

/* PlainHeaderView paints itself, so ::section styling cannot reach it; these
   qproperty hooks are how a themed header gets its colours (see the class note
   in FileListView.cpp). The ::section rule below still covers any plain
   QHeaderView elsewhere in the app. */
/* Matched by class name, NOT by the QHeaderView base: Qt applies a
   qproperty- rule to every widget the selector matches and warns on stderr for
   each one that lacks the property, so a plain `QHeaderView` selector produces
   a warning per ordinary header (dialogs, tree views) on every polish. */
PlainHeaderView {
    qproperty-sectionBackground: #0a1a0d;
    qproperty-sectionForeground: #33ff88;
    qproperty-sectionBorder: #1fa85c;
}

QHeaderView::section {
    background-color: #0a1a0d;
    color: #33ff88;
    padding: 4px;
    border: none;
    border-right: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    font-weight: normal;
}

QLineEdit {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 3px 6px;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

QLineEdit:focus {
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
}

/* No border at rest: BreadcrumbBar's own paintable area sits inside
   PanelAddressRow, which already owns the address toolbar's left/right box
   lines (AddressRowBorderOverlay in BreadcrumbBar.cpp) spanning the tree
   toggle, back/forward, breadcrumb and star button as one row. A permanent
   border here duplicated that -- BreadcrumbBar occupies most of the row's
   width (it is the only stretchy child), so its own top/bottom edges read as
   a second, misaligned border across the whole row, and its own left edge
   (well inside the row, past the fixed-width buttons) read as the overlay's
   left line failing to reach the bottom. Only the editing state gets a
   border, as a "you're now typing a path" cue distinct from the row's own
   box and the QLineEdit's focus ring. */
BreadcrumbBar[editing="true"] {
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
    border-radius: 0;
}

BreadcrumbBar QWidget#BreadcrumbDisplay,
BreadcrumbBar QWidget#BreadcrumbSegments,
BreadcrumbBar QWidget#BreadcrumbViewport {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
}

BreadcrumbBar QLineEdit {
    border: none;
    border-radius: 0;
    margin: 0;
}

BreadcrumbBar QToolButton#BreadcrumbScrollLeft,
BreadcrumbBar QToolButton#BreadcrumbScrollRight {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
}

/* CommandBar owns the chrome tile. Its only input reveals that parent surface,
   preserving one phase across prompt and editor while retaining this control's
   border, text, cursor and selection palette. */
CommandBar QLineEdit#CommandInput {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 0;
}

QAbstractSpinBox {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 3px 20px 3px 6px;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

QAbstractSpinBox:hover {
    border-color: #1fa85c;
}

QAbstractSpinBox:focus {
    border-color: #33ff88;
}

QAbstractSpinBox:disabled {
    background-color: #071108;
    color: #12602f;
    border-color: #12602f;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button, QAbstractSpinBox::down-button {
    subcontrol-origin: border;
    width: 18px;
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    border-left: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button {
    subcontrol-position: top right;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-button {
    subcontrol-position: bottom right;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:hover, QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
}

QComboBox {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 2px 6px;
}

QComboBox:focus {
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
}

QComboBox:hover {
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

QComboBox::drop-down {
    subcontrol-origin: padding;
    subcontrol-position: center right;
    width: 18px;
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
}

QComboBox QAbstractItemView {
    background-color: #0a1a0d;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    outline: none;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

/* Spelled out on ::item for the same DTK reason documented in dark.qss. */
QComboBox QAbstractItemView::item {
    color: #33ff88;
    padding: 3px 8px;
    min-height: 20px;
}

QComboBox QAbstractItemView::item:selected {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}

/* Buttons read as key caps: unlit face, lit border, inverted when pressed. */
QPushButton {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 5px 10px;
}

QPushButton:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
}

QPushButton:pressed {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}

QPushButton:disabled {
    color: #12602f;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
}

QToolButton {
    background: transparent;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 3px 6px;
}

QToolButton:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
}

QToolButton:pressed {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}

QTableWidget#ShortcutTable QKeySequenceEdit,
QTableWidget#ShortcutTable QKeySequenceEdit QLineEdit {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
}

/* The tab-add control keeps the CRT chrome tile at rest. */
QToolButton#PanelAddTabButton {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
}

QToolButton#PanelAddTabButton:hover {
    background: #12602f;
    border: none;
}

QToolButton#PanelAddTabButton:pressed {
    background: #0a3f1f;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: none;
}

QToolButton#PanelAddTabButton:disabled {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #12602f;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton {
    background: transparent;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:hover {
    background: #12602f;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:pressed {
    background: #0a3f1f;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:disabled {
    background: transparent;
    color: #12602f;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:checked {
    background: #1fa85c;
    color: #04140a;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:checked:hover {
    background: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#PanelAddressRow QToolButton:checked:pressed {
    background: #147a42;
    color: #04140a;
    border: none;
}

TitleBar QToolButton#TitleMenuButton {
    padding: 4px 8px;
}

TitleBar QToolButton#TitleMenuButton::menu-indicator {
    image: none;
}

QTabBar::tab {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #1fa85c;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-bottom: none;
    padding: 5px 12px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}

/* TabBar paints its accent and close controls from the explicit state colours
   ThemeManager supplies alongside this sheet. Keep the tab face itself flat,
   but brighten the label because inactive tabs use the half-lit green. */
QTabBar::tab:selected {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QTabBar::tab:hover {
    color: #33ff88;
}

QTabBar QToolButton {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    padding: 0;
}

QTabBar QToolButton:hover {
    background: #12602f;
    border-color: #1fa85c;
}

QTabBar QToolButton:pressed {
    background: #0a3f1f;
    color: #33ff88;
    border-color: #1fa85c;
}

QTabBar QToolButton:disabled {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #12602f;
    border-color: #12602f;
}

QStatusBar {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    border-top: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QStatusBar::item {
    border: none;
}

QProgressBar {
    background-color: #04140a;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    border-radius: 0;
    text-align: center;
    color: #33ff88;
}

/* A blocky, stepped fill rather than one smooth bar -- the chunk is drawn in
   segments by giving it a margin, which is what makes it look like discrete
   character cells filling up. */
QProgressBar::chunk {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    width: 8px;
    margin: 1px;
}

QMessageBox {
    background-color: #071108;
}

QLabel {
    background: transparent;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QCheckBox, QRadioButton {
    color: #33ff88;
    background: transparent;
}

QCheckBox::indicator, QRadioButton::indicator {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    border: 1px solid #1fa85c;
    background: #04140a;
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked, QRadioButton::indicator:checked {
    background: #33ff88;
}

QGroupBox {
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    margin-top: 8px;
    padding-top: 6px;
}

QGroupBox::title {
    subcontrol-origin: margin;
    left: 8px;
    padding: 0 4px;
    color: #33ff88;
}

QSplitter::handle {
    background-color: #12602f;
}

QPlainTextEdit, QTextEdit {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

/* Square scrollbars with no rounding, so the trough reads as a channel. */
QScrollBar:vertical {
    width: 12px;
    background: #04140a;
    border: none;
}

QScrollBar:horizontal {
    height: 12px;
    background: #04140a;
    border: none;
}

QScrollBar::handle:vertical {
    background: #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    min-height: 24px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:horizontal {
    background: #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    min-width: 24px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:hover {
    background: #1fa85c;
}

QScrollBar::add-page, QScrollBar::sub-page {
    background: #04140a;
}

QScrollBar::add-line:vertical, QScrollBar::sub-line:vertical {
    height: 0;
    background: #04140a;
}

QScrollBar::add-line:horizontal, QScrollBar::sub-line:horizontal {
    width: 0;
    background: #04140a;
}

QTreeView {
    background-color: #04140a;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    alternate-background-color: #071a0d;
    selection-background-color: #33ff88;
    selection-color: #04140a;
}

/* Hover has to stay in the phosphor hue: the platform style's own hover fill is
   a neutral grey/blue, which reads as a second colour on this theme. */
QTreeView::item:hover:!selected {
    background-color: #12602f;
    color: #33ff88;
}

/* Branch arrows are phosphor green too; gray would introduce a second hue. */
QTreeView::branch:has-children:!has-siblings:closed,
QTreeView::branch:closed:has-children:has-siblings {
    border-image: none;
    image: url(:/icons/branch-closed-green.svg);
}
QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:!has-siblings,
QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:has-siblings {
    border-image: none;
    image: url(:/icons/branch-open-green.svg);
}

QToolTip {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-chrome.png) repeat;
    color: #33ff88;
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 3px;
}

/* External-connection fly-out. Square top corners here too: the rounded
   treatment the other themes use is exactly the thing a CRT theme should not
   have. The bottom border is still dropped so it merges with its button. */
#ExternalConnectPanel {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-screen.png) repeat;
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
    border-bottom: none;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList {
    background-color: #04140a;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    outline: none;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList::item {
    padding: 4px 6px;
    border-radius: 0;
    color: #33ff88;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList::item:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList::item:selected {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList QScrollBar:vertical {
    width: 7px;
    background: transparent;
    margin: 0;
}
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList QScrollBar::add-line:vertical,
#ExternalConnectPanel QListWidget#ExternalConnectList QScrollBar::sub-line:vertical {
    height: 0;
    background: transparent;
}

/* Quick-notepad fly-out: same treatment. */
#NotepadPanel {
    background: url(:/icons/crt-scan-screen.png) repeat;
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
    border-bottom: none;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList {
    background-color: #04140a;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    outline: none;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList::item {
    padding: 4px 6px;
    border-radius: 0;
    color: #33ff88;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList::item:hover {
    background-color: #12602f;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList::item:selected {
    background-color: #33ff88;
    color: #04140a;
}
#NotepadPanel QPlainTextEdit#NotepadEditor {
    background-color: #04140a;
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
    color: #33ff88;
}
#NotepadPanel QSplitter#NotepadSplitter::handle {
    background-color: #12602f;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList QScrollBar:vertical {
    width: 7px;
    background: transparent;
    margin: 0;
}
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList QScrollBar::add-line:vertical,
#NotepadPanel QListWidget#NotepadList QScrollBar::sub-line:vertical {
    height: 0;
    background: transparent;
}

/* See light.qss: the frame belongs to the bar, not to the editor inside it. */
CommandBar {
    border: 1px solid #12602f;
    border-radius: 0;
}

CommandBar[inputFocused="true"] {
    border: 1px solid #33ff88;
}
